{"id":"idolatry","name":"Idolatry","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:idolatry","torrey:idolatry"],"groups":["Wicked Practices Of","Other Customs Of","Objects Of"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Idolatry","text":"Image-worship or divine honour paid to any created object. Paul describes the origin of idolatry in Rom. 1:21-25: men forsook God, and sank into ignorance and moral corruption (1:28). The forms of idolatry are, (1.) Fetishism, or the worship of trees, rivers, hills, stones, etc. (2.) Nature worship, the worship of the sun, moon, and stars, as the supposed powers of nature. (3.) Hero worship, the worship of deceased ancestors, or of heroes. In Scripture, idolatry is regarded as of heathen origin, and as being imported among the Hebrews through contact with heathen nations. The first allusion to idolatry is in the account of Rachel stealing her father’s teraphim (Gen. 31:19), which were the relics of the worship of other gods by Laban’s progenitors “on the other side of the river in old time” (Josh. 24:2). During their long residence in Egypt the Hebrews fell into idolatry, and it was …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H7812","lemma":"שָׁחָה","xlit":"shâchâh","gloss":"to depress","kjv":"bow  down, crouch, fall down, humbly beseech","count":43,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H5647","lemma":"עָבַד","xlit":"ʻâbad","gloss":"to work","kjv":"be, keep in bondage, be bondmen, bond-service","count":46,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H312","lemma":"אַחֵר","xlit":"ʼachêr","gloss":"hinder","kjv":"other man, following, next, strange","count":39,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H2181","lemma":"זָנָה","xlit":"zânâh","gloss":"to commit adultery","kjv":"commit fornication, continually, great, harlot","count":34,"share":0.04},{"strong":"H1544","lemma":"גִּלּוּל","xlit":"gillûwl","gloss":"a log","kjv":"idol","count":31,"share":0.04},{"strong":"H6999","lemma":"קָטַר","xlit":"qâṭar","gloss":"to smoke","kjv":"burn, incense, kindle, offer","count":28,"share":0.03}],"verses"